    "title": "Verify a new-hire I-9 document review before the deadline",
    "description": "A practical human resources teams guide for HR leaders, people operations teams, and growing companies, focused on I-9 document reviews with explicit evidence, decision rights, and a verified close.",
    "category": "Workflow",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-07",
    "readTime": "10 min read",
    "thumbnail": "/thumbnails/november-07/hrcoordinators-verify-a-new-hire-i-9-document-review-before-the-deadline.webp",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "The control this I-9 document reviews needs",
        "paragraphs": [
          "HR Coordinators treats I-9 document reviews as a control problem rather than a speed problem. The work begins when a source record enters the I-9 document reviews queue, not when someone remembers to check it. A coordinator prepares the evidence, while the accountable HR owner keeps the decision that changes a commitment or an employee record. A reviewer should be able to reconstruct the case without asking the coordinator who did the work. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews reviewer checks the approved I-9 document reviews record against the same standard before the next shift.",
          "The first gap in I-9 document reviews is usually a missing link between the source and the decision. A queue can look current while an I-9 document reviews case is missing the form, the date, or the approval that makes it reviewable. HR Coordinators asks the team to record the source before the case moves forward. That habit separates activity from a control that a later reviewer can audit. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews note stays with the employee file so a later HR owner can reopen it without rebuilding the history."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "What the I-9 document reviews record must show",
        "paragraphs": [
          "A useful I-9 document reviews record answers four questions. Who opened the case, what source was checked, which field changed, and who approved the close. Those answers belong together in the I-9 document reviews record, not in a separate email thread. A reviewer should be able to follow the path in under a minute. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews owner reads the same fields before signing off.",
          "The evidence standard for I-9 document reviews is deliberately modest. Record the source with the date it was observed, and mark a missing value as missing. Do not fill a gap from memory because a later reviewer will treat the invented value as fact. HR Coordinators keeps the original source and the correction note side by side. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews correction is dated and initialed."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where the support role stops",
        "paragraphs": [
          "The coordinator may prepare, check, record, and route. The HR owner keeps any decision that changes pay, access, policy, an employee commitment, or a personnel record. When a case raises an emergency, a privacy question, a dispute, or a request outside the written scope, preserve the facts and route it. A deliberate stop protects the employee record when the exception is real. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews stop reason is written in plain language.",
          "Do not solve a I-9 document reviews coverage gap with a shared login. Give each coordinator an individual account with only the permissions required for the written scope. Remove those permissions when the assignment changes or ends. The access record is part of the control, not an IT afterthought. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews owner confirms the access change before the next cycle."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "The daily I-9 document reviews routine",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Start the I-9 document reviews routine with the oldest unresolved item, not the newest message. Open the source, confirm the employee and the request, and check the last verified fact. Then record the next action with an owner and a time. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews queue is reviewed before the first scheduled meeting.",
          "End the day by reconciling the I-9 document reviews queue and explaining the carryover. The handoff should name what moved, what is waiting, who owns the next check, and which evidence is still missing. Keep the queue short enough to finish; a healthy average can hide one aged case. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews handoff note is stored with the employee record."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Exceptions that should stop the case",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Predictable I-9 document reviews exceptions include a missing source, two records that disagree, an expired authorization, an unavailable owner, unsigned forms, or a request outside the written scope. Record the exception and the exact missing field so the next reviewer does not repeat the search. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews exception code is chosen from a short approved list. The note stays with the case until the owner resolves it.",
          "If a I-9 document reviews exception touches a payroll date, an access grant, a privacy question, or an employee commitment, the dependent action waits for the accountable HR owner. Do not infer approval from silence or from a prior similar case. The resolution should say what was decided and where the evidence lives. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews resolution is dated and linked to the source."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to verify and close",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Verification for I-9 document reviews means checking the destination record, not the activity log. A sent message or a closed task proves that someone acted, but it does not prove that the employee record or the access state changed. Confirm the field or outcome the work was supposed to affect. If the destination cannot be checked, the case is not complete. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews close note names the record that was inspected.",
          "A close note should say what was checked, who approved it, what changed, and what would reopen the case. The HR owner should be able to read one record later and understand why it closed without contacting the original coordinator. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews close note is kept with the source evidence. The note should not restate the whole history."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "The weekly I-9 document reviews handoff",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Set one recurring point each week to review the I-9 document reviews queue: the oldest items, the blocked items, and one case that was corrected. Keep the meeting short and decision-focused. Use the same sample rule every week so a change can be compared. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews review names a single process change and a check date.",
          "Record the exact source for I-9 document reviews and the time it was observed. A later reviewer should be able to tell whether the value was current when the first action was taken. When the source is maintained by another team, name the contact and the expected response time. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews escalation path is written next to the queue."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "The monthly I-9 document reviews review",
        "paragraphs": [
          "Once a month, pull a small I-9 document reviews sample and test the source, the decision, the execution, and the confirmation against the same rule. Include one normal case, one exception, and one case that was reopened. End with one process change, one owner, and one check date. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews sample is selected from the queue, not by the coordinator being reviewed.",
          "If the same I-9 document reviews defect appears twice, change the template or the source before coaching the person again. Remove unused fields so the record stays short enough to complete under pressure. Keep the review note with the process change it produced. At HR Coordinators, the I-9 document reviews review is repeated on a fixed date."
        ]
      }
    ],
